14`.\n\n**Recommendation**: Use `AscendC::CrossCoreSetFlag<MODE, PIPE>(flagId)` paired with `AscendC::CrossCoreWaitFlag(flagId)` as an explicit stage handoff. On V220/950PR examples, `CrossCoreWaitFlag` is the untemplated wait form. For `KERNEL_TYPE_MIX_AIC_1_2`, use `MODE = 0x2`, the AIC<->AIV paired sync mode. This orders paired sub-block participants; it is not whole-device broadcast and not a `SyncAll<true>()` replacement.\n\nPipe selection:\n- Use `PIPE_FIX` when AIC produces data through FIX to GM.\n- Use `PIPE_MTE3` when AIV produces data through MTE3 to GM.\n- Choose the pipe whose retirement must precede the consumer read.\n- Do not use a compute pipe when the dependency is a GM transfer; releasing before the GM transfer retires can expose stale data.\n\nFlag ownership:\n- User data flags are `0..FFTS_MAX_FLAG` = `0..7`.\n- IDs `8`, `9`, and `10` are reserved for barrier forms in the verified header; do not allocate them for user-owned data handoffs.\n- Existing canonical KB records same-flag max count / reverse-depth budget as 15. Do not design a ping-pong or counted protocol requiring more than 15 outstanding same-flag generations without separate source-level proof for that exact build.\n- Long pipelines must reuse IDs only after the prior handoff is known consumed, or must use a separately verified counted/reverse protocol.\n\nConcrete anchor:\n```cpp\nconstexpr uint32_t cubeOutReadyId = 1;\nconstexpr uint32_t vecOutReadyId  = 2;\n\n#ifdef __DAV_C220_CUBE__\n// AIC stage: after cube output through FIX is complete/ordered for handoff.\nAscendC::CrossCoreSetFlag<0x2, PIPE_FIX>(cubeOutReadyId);\n#endif\n\n#ifdef __DAV_C220_VEC__\n// AIV stage: wait before reading the cube intermediate.\nAscendC::CrossCoreWaitFlag(cubeOutReadyId);\n// ... vector compute writes its intermediate to GM through MTE3 ...\nAscendC::CrossCoreSetFlag<0x2, PIPE_MTE3>(vecOutReadyId);\n#endif\n\n#ifdef __DAV_C220_CUBE__\n// Later AIC stage: wait before reading the vector intermediate.